|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/ Here's a C++ function that uses the markdown library to convert markdown text to HTML:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <markdown.h>

/**
 * Converts markdown text to HTML.
 *
 * @param markdownText The markdown text to convert.
 * @return The converted HTML text.
 */
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdownText) {
    markdown::MarkdownIn markdown(markdownText);
    markdown::MarkdownOut htmlOut;

    markdown.render(&markdown, &htmlOut);

    return htmlOut.c_str();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Hello, World!";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);

    std::cout << htmlText << std::endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses the markdown library to convert markdown text to HTML. The `markdownToHtml` function takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown text, converts it to HTML, and returns the result as a `std::string`. The `main` function demonstrates how to use this function.

Please note that you need to install the markdown library before compiling this code. You can install it using your package manager or by downloading it from the official website.

Also, make sure to link against the markdown library when compiling. The exact command will depend on your compiler and the installation location of the markdown library.

For example, with g++:

```bash
g++ -o markdown_to_html markdown_to_html.cpp -lmarkdown
```

